\frac{3}{10})}^{x+1}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/5f/5f4c0e3c4573f6e3167390d54ee21bbb8bf6e3879fcc06e7adf9d311e80ccb62.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{3}{10})}^{x+1}.$ The point $(-1,1)$ moves to $(-1,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/b5/b541282b8661f2fb28d0ce1f10c22a9a8cc7b083460c05093a89722bc084d7b9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb183013-a42d-50a3-a1b8-cd8501781075" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial With Multiple Variables as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17f420e0-2ce0-5aca-8dee-2fc02f4be838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+{y}^{3})({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297656} {:answer/id #uuid "2decc769-5d34-52a4-8f61-0d917cf2c0e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{5}+{y}^{2})(x-{y}^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297746} {:answer/id #uuid "84d3cf7b-8bb1-5c05-bad0-4556bf860b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298125} {:answer/id #uuid "903c4fc8-2bfb-5749-a8dc-9a4f1681fb2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})({x}^{3}-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298178} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0ab2a9-e83d-55df-8e55-44c8b207e2a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17f420e0-2ce0-5aca-8dee-2fc02f4be838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+{y}^{3})({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297656} :answer-field/id #uuid "710ade4f-6788-57df-bfae-27eab8eaa863" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a03ef93a-a766-5bc2-9cd5-d8ee9f70bca4" :question/math-academy-id "q-31991" :question/problem "Factor ${x}^{4}-{y}^{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{y}^{6}={({x}^{2})}^{2}-{({y}^{3})}^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{({x}^{2})}^{2}-{({y}^{3})}^{2}=({x}^{2}+{y}^{3})({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115401 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c1def23-83e0-5dc7-b20b-9693127d3a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Inverse Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006c4d37-009e-5065-aa0e-e789bc12caf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{|8-{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{(8-{x}^{4})^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297551} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a34a37-aaa2-5b33-8413-e4f969d084a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12{x}^{3}}{|8-{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{(8-{x}^{4})^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298270} {:answer/id #uuid "bb221971-81aa-5b2d-a077-c6b670f32b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{|8-{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{(8-{x}^{4})^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298367} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e98b1b-1585-5bcb-a6c7-f9e80aef1351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{|8-{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{(8-{x}^{4})^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298401} {:answer/id #uuid "cd44316a-87ed-548b-af75-38a85154bbc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12{x}^{3}}{|8-{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{(8-{x}^{4})^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb221971-81aa-5b2d-a077-c6b670f32b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{|8-{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{(8-{x}^{4})^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298367} :answer-field/id #uuid "b39d87eb-5482-5669-84b6-1f25fa680a00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5f8a50f-d033-5259-86d9-bade982764d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-32000" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=2arccsc(8-{x}^{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of $\\text{arccsc}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\text{arccsc}\\,x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2arccsc(8-{x}^{4})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arccsc(8-{x}^{4})) \\\\  & =2(-\\frac{1}{|8-{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{(8-{x}^{4})^{2}-1}})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8-{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =2(-\\frac{1}{|8-{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{(8-{x}^{4})^{2}-1}})\\cdot (-4{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{|8-{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{(8-{x}^{4})^{2}-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092183 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c89778fa-3c8d-5f0a-a0f8-c9b3d9193ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using an Inverse Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2cf0dd5-0db7-57a2-9870-1611db6da665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390713} {:answer/id #uuid "be6ce68f-4c42-58da-863b-b22d6376ce30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390714} {:answer/id #uuid "333a570b-447e-5d5d-9439-65c7fcbb64c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390715} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5e534f-499c-5a4d-9bd0-1ecbfeee8157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390716} {:answer/id #uuid "f518abcf-bf8c-56aa-bf15-d5e00e0d770d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae5e534f-499c-5a4d-9bd0-1ecbfeee8157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390716} :answer-field/id #uuid "89bea882-0921-5708-a9f9-9d676afb881e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ffd8425a-ff52-5722-b741-7d19394751e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-32136" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=-1 \\\\ 2x+3y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092183 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c89778fa-3c8d-5f0a-a0f8-c9b3d9193ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using an Inverse Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af53f340-3e36-5545-937e-11e307962671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}46 \\\\ -23\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390720} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ec4ea9-2509-5c2c-a216-2db5287d79fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{46}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}46 \\\\ -23\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390721} {:answer/id #uuid "99392c81-e8f0-5fb5-ae4c-99f2a069feb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}46 \\\\ -23\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390722} {:answer/id #uuid "bff6331c-295a-540c-a6d8-28d96895301d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{46}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}46 \\\\ -23\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390723} {:answer/id #uuid "4d16e9a1-1407-53d0-b940-8956dc7a7aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{46}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}46 \\\\ -23\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d16e9a1-1407-53d0-b940-8956dc7a7aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{46}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}46 \\\\ -23\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390724} :answer-field/id #uuid "94a86967-5a41-5f2d-8c19-38b625daba3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34c426c5-7657-5020-93fd-26e20c441212" :question/math-academy-id "q-32139" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+6y=46 \\\\ 8x+y=-23\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}46 \\\\ -23\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1-6\\cdot 8}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{46}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{46}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}46 \\\\ -23\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119940 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0305491f-5ac7-5ba4-9a80-1a9a79b964f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating dy/dx at a Point Using dx/dy"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2104e133-914f-5ccf-9abb-bb338526ec6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300116} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7b2fae-8ac3-57a0-bffe-05751f19e1e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{54}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300187} {:answer/id #uuid "9e343b3d-6257-5d65-9a7e-1c0b02359997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300760} {:answer/id #uuid "a782896a-abec-59e4-9290-6a432fe3815a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300805} {:answer/id #uuid "bebe977a-dc05-5a44-8579-fd4c6afc897a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bebe977a-dc05-5a44-8579-fd4c6afc897a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300914} :answer-field/id #uuid "38691da1-c3cd-5bcd-bc71-b36d06760bd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e944b722-89ed-5df5-8b31-cb3570eaaf1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-32595" :question/problem "If $x=2\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(5,9).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{y})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{1/2})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}-3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-3/2}) \\\\  & ={y}^{-1/2}+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}+\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}+\\frac{3}{2y\\sqrt{y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y+3}{2y\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{2y\\sqrt{y}}{2y+3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(5,9),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(5,9)} & =\\frac{2(9)\\sqrt{9}}{2(9)+3}=\\frac{18}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119941 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ff302ba-5a3f-599b-b038-7417af1dca71" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Tangent Line Using dx/dy"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d73b7d-9403-55ab-b551-e19830698e26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{12}+\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300052} {:answer/id #uuid "6a33eeae-06c8-52d2-b05c-f45a9e998a06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{12}+\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300498} {:answer/id #uuid "9e0ef0fa-a60f-528b-b723-df752ee1c670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{12}+\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300758} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d76d1c-be51-51c6-8cde-9ba71e9a668a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=9x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300855} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e2c41b-b138-5e44-b020-de7bc94b2004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=12x-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16d73b7d-9403-55ab-b551-e19830698e26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{12}+\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300052} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a3602d1-8784-558b-8e8c-d79f5c14e514" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7ef2446-7ddb-53cb-83cf-b4fed6b4ea6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-32596" :question/problem "Given a curve $x=\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2},$ what is the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point $(9,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3{y}^{2}-2y \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}-2y}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(9,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(9,3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(3)^{2}-2(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{12}(x-9) \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{12}-\\frac{3}{4}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{12}+\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119978 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c0b7d95-c699-5762-aa44-449712f035c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "319c0fb3-aee5-5e2d-bcbf-a1949dbc941d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{g}^{-1}(x)+6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300206} {:answer/id #uuid "896ccbe6-a82d-5b23-9d7c-b0f373d22b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{g}^{-1}(x)+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300656} {:answer/id #uuid "da6d18f3-3cee-525c-86e4-e352d45bb12f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4{g}^{-1}(x)+6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301044} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c1668d-0e47-5c9f-bcd8-84e9ad090306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4{g}^{-1}(x)+6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301161} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0f86e0-2705-5cc9-b536-c3f797b75455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2({g}^{-1}(x))+6{g}^{-1}(x)-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2c1668d-0e47-5c9f-bcd8-84e9ad090306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4{g}^{-1}(x)+6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301161} :answer-field/id #uuid "348b05f8-ac66-5d57-a5c3-05e956512cbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62e6ddbb-e71d-53d4-9a83-95cdcae2804b" :question/math-academy-id "q-32629" :question/problem "If $g(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x-2,$ find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=2{x}^{2}+6x-2,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=2{y}^{2}+6y-2\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+6y-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =4y+6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{4y+6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:**\n\nLastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{4{g}^{-1}(x)+6} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4{g}^{-1}(x)+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0196af83-9ef6-5bab-9f50-0aacf488f0cc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Composite Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e68e0e6-041a-534e-ae3f-656e3055c656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300186} {:answer/id #uuid "3d41be64-6ef0-5cb2-8c49-dfdb88df1918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300260} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed454bc-d138-582d-9b57-ad9bfb532c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300270} {:answer/id #uuid "77e1d8bb-f34a-54b5-88c5-760321bf0433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300552} {:answer/id #uuid "7f545b1d-f3a2-5eae-aa1d-a60253aadbbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d41be64-6ef0-5cb2-8c49-dfdb88df1918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300260} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6625e80-6f3e-5f94-979a-72f6d57fbe39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2318ec9c-3e92-5221-b4ff-d2d8a492f82d" :question/math-academy-id "q-32635" :question/problem "If $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+5)-1,$ find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+5)-1,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y+5)-1\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y+5)-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y+5)\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y+5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y+5)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2y+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)+5) \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119939 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00ac6edc-908a-50be-9ef1-4b24ba597d1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating dy/dx Using dx/dy"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "233da062-0046-5ff6-be71-aee3cc7e71f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4+4{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300130} {:answer/id #uuid "95d1ecb0-3160-5adc-97f9-910e8d9c239c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{3}}{1+{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300719} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a94346-e82d-5c11-b3c7-77080761ef66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300843} {:answer/id #uuid "f6dc110a-2fc4-5a9e-a3d2-5fed13a83b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{4+4{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301179} {:answer/id #uuid "fae0bb00-a872-5722-aa1c-2b78582a5353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4+4{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "233da062-0046-5ff6-be71-aee3cc7e71f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4+4{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300130} :answer-field/id #uuid "84d7d2bf-ce4d-5963-b71f-6932bfd6b3ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd82fd71-e11f-52b4-879b-d6f659d732bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-32705" :question/problem "If $x=-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}+2{y}^{2},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}+2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-2{y}^{-2}+2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-2})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =4{y}^{-3}+4y \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{y}^{3}}+4y \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4{y}^{4}}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4+4{y}^{4}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119978 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c0b7d95-c699-5762-aa44-449712f035c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad77982-9d97-5298-9445-aa2f29bc75ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302529} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2f1231-6fab-51a9-b30c-1f9c29aba694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302837} {:answer/id #uuid "89a7c6b9-61e0-5fa5-b9cf-bcfa192e6661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303314} {:answer/id #uuid "97905197-8f84-5b48-bca4-efd82fb4afb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303397} {:answer/id #uuid "eba8ac9e-26d2-5904-bec3-e057833f71f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d2f1231-6fab-51a9-b30c-1f9c29aba694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302837} :answer-field/id #uuid "67848d2d-ef17-55d4-8334-d4361a471cbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54994de7-137e-54bf-80bb-ec8f86707073" :question/math-academy-id "q-3300" :question/problem "Find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$  as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x),$ if $g(x)=4{x}^{3}+2x+6.$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=4{x}^{3}+2x+6,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=4{y}^{3}+2y+6\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4{y}^{3}+2y+6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =12{y}^{2}+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{12{y}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "252d775f-77ed-511b-95ad-524d7bb4cd39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315326} {:answer/id #uuid "6bcab351-5c37-5d1b-99d8-985db29eacd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315726} {:answer/id #uuid "969cb243-da0c-5f4c-8e30-aedc2baa4e6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants III and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315954} {:answer/id #uuid "ae942c7b-d556-576d-99b4-c2d5e0d5ad8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316110} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b0d548-2819-5f8e-b792-8ccfa0fb789c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "969cb243-da0c-5f4c-8e30-aedc2baa4e6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants III and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315954} :answer-field/id #uuid "e94ae561-6bac-5708-8ab7-f7ca8354b9fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8483a20c-9e8e-5588-94ee-dd0e0014806e" :question/math-academy-id "q-34325" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =-{450}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{450}^{∘}$ by *adding* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- $-{450}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}=-90$\n\n- $-{450}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={270}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${270}^{∘}$ is coterminal with $-{450}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/35/35b9783c9ac87b0a8d177e7bdf340b48513146404b7c768a8532da59b6ec7948.png)\n\nWe see that ${270}^{∘}$ lies between quadrants III and IV. Therefore, $-{450}^{∘}$ also lies between these quadrants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7f04ab7-f6f6-5d01-bfcf-b667e37e4f0b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Irrational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "824db7a0-468b-5b7c-bdd3-7bd19185dd56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 80, z =-5 ± 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066779} {:answer/id #uuid "f44af2a5-1591-5f5e-93ba-40c0ddd584b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 80, z =-8 ± 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066780} {:answer/id #uuid "30dfca08-4199-59da-8d02-2600e0f2122b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 75, z = 5 ± 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066781} {:answer/id #uuid "4a3b9749-a33c-5c57-89ac-26939f855dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 80, z = 5 ± 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066782} {:answer/id #uuid "6d135e5e-ebb4-5c27-84bf-0be68ee6c029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q =-70, z = ± \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066783}] :answer-field/id #uuid "988361f0-82f8-5fa0-bce8-8775fead2e27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066778} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eb9e11c-6e9c-5d9f-85b6-41a705579cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=80,\\,z=-5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402357} {:answer/id #uuid "23289d14-87e9-57fb-bbda-cdd305eb9ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-70,\\,z=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402358} {:answer/id #uuid "691a3cdd-d3d5-50e7-a708-646cfa31e0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=80,\\,z=-8\\pm 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402359} {:answer/id #uuid "823c0a9e-ef4b-5a9d-a5eb-0fe274767e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=75,\\,z=5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402360} {:answer/id #uuid "ee362d99-5b0e-55c5-83d5-a9a8872163ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=80,\\,z=5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9eb9e11c-6e9c-5d9f-85b6-41a705579cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=80,\\,z=-5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402357} :answer-field/id #uuid "0174c191-2a23-5d0f-b8d7-9880c6f7ee41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c0076f5-08a5-5d4c-9789-196650ab8295" :question/math-academy-id "q-3435" :question/problem "The equation ${z}^{2}-50=-10z$ can be written in the form $(z+p{)}^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, the $-10z$ term to the left-hand side, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-50 & =-10z \\\\ {z}^{2}+10z & =50 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z & =50 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2} & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=5,q=75,$ and $p+q=80.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+5{)}^{2} & =75 \\\\ \\sqrt{(z+5{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\ |z+5| & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\ z+5 & =\\pm 5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ z & =-5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f1000f1-7874-54ec-a25c-a6fdd4c99c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058255} {:answer/id #uuid "3d05c2d3-5f8b-520c-9698-93cd44016078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058256} {:answer/id #uuid "3e675e47-ccc8-5f07-88dd-4486ea14690b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058257} {:answer/id #uuid "4077c45f-7284-5f8a-b112-9435133409d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058258} {:answer/id #uuid "73d4409a-0928-587b-a8b0-c3b59584d36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058259}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ba6c658f-3e45-5acf-b1fd-cc6fc99efea5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058254} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f053f6c2-d473-5f4a-9761-a34e3f8fa5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402370} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c1d7ef-187c-5c23-afd5-1de27fa281b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402371} {:answer/id #uuid "e2833382-6b90-5b3c-ae32-77694f3ceaef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402372} {:answer/id #uuid "79867f36-2c63-53a2-b0d7-906e247536ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402373} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1cfc22-b026-5ac0-aed5-a3a643bfff05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2833382-6b90-5b3c-ae32-77694f3ceaef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402372} :answer-field/id #uuid "88bbe855-4ccb-5da3-a31c-9c9bb8b0b176" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80f5801f-10e6-5373-bb84-2300dad1ed58" :question/math-academy-id "q-34403" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ by *adding* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n\n- $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/89/891959642b0d055d852329c59be7541179dd5ae0c4182c2bec533261ce3a700d.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ also lies in this quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53025447-2eb1-5df5-a00f-6e8c8a103513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{89}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319630} {:answer/id #uuid "66db5717-8d6c-56e3-9e6e-8159327ca744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{67}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319706} {:answer/id #uuid "6f875b91-463b-5b9e-b642-9334220d3e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319727} {:answer/id #uuid "78093d05-d766-51e5-bfbd-15a4b1ad83e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{67}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319753} {:answer/id #uuid "b538e599-0c29-5367-95db-1d80a51d71e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{81}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b538e599-0c29-5367-95db-1d80a51d71e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{81}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319948} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c1dc974-537c-5a19-8b0e-de4ef3bf427a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b2f4df1-3e4e-5541-bb2f-d2b7890aaf73" :question/math-academy-id "q-35079" :question/problem "Rewrite $-2{z}^{2}-14z+16$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({z}^{2}+7z-8)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+7z-8\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}\\cdot z-8\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-8\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{81}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({z}^{2}+7z-8) & =-2({(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{81}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105854 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ff9d00-cfe3-5000-9f39-f5b084d00080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319455} {:answer/id #uuid "9035921e-6fcf-5772-b9a5-7d9a496b6106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319823} {:answer/id #uuid "d6df4228-d351-594e-9eea-e865f3a883bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320046} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc970a1-4ea5-589f-8352-ce90a07452c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320063} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a74ebb-b1db-5cf0-a20e-5165336efc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6df4228-d351-594e-9eea-e865f3a883bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320046} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cb04ca6-e219-5d5e-ab67-487f7d66d988" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c7529a5-2ffe-53f0-9bca-cd9b5d97a802" :question/math-academy-id "q-35192" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{5}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-\\frac{1}{4}$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5} & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4})=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40a70c8d-dc8e-52bd-a679-ffd6d1940451" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Function With a Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9b836c-fae2-50e0-a795-3a27cfdd5785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+a}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319421} {:answer/id #uuid "3492000c-c2fa-51a4-bcee-24c414d9ca52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=7x-a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319554} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3d6dcf-2ec9-5287-a8ef-e92f9f472281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-7}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319812} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb04d9c-7b29-5181-b95e-06fc65228ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+7}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320017} {:answer/id #uuid "d00e46ee-e183-5993-a320-dcbaa9aa4eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-a}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccb04d9c-7b29-5181-b95e-06fc65228ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+7}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320017} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5d4e30f-32ac-5460-b456-38e5a87775f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "abfc488e-a974-5dbc-b40c-2f281295336b" :question/math-academy-id "q-35217" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=ax-7$, where $a$ is a non-zero constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=ax-7\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=ay-7\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =ay-7 \\\\ x+7 & =ay \\\\ \\frac{x+7}{a} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+7}{a}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ccc7bce-8234-5ee1-bd8b-2bcdd3fcbd07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319574} {:answer/id #uuid "574ba857-9f96-590d-abdc-bb096d802847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319648} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7a7fe3-5133-5ba3-baf2-2c6b2e00ce1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319916} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c8a647-b5b3-5af4-85b7-661bdc068c81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320074} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b296d9-adc9-527b-a4b4-ccaabe649e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ccc7bce-8234-5ee1-bd8b-2bcdd3fcbd07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319574} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e14ff5e-6a4f-5e24-8876-c8a24ddf7181" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ffa1d84-420d-51e5-b5d5-2102976566da" :question/math-academy-id "q-35243" :question/problem "![](images/67/67b77d2d3798e87593c7dddeea93603fff36d9153f53248cc2dab295f4879321.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule and the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x))}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=3.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{3}$ does not change the sign of the limit. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2})={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40a70c8d-dc8e-52bd-a679-ffd6d1940451" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Function With a Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28a8e3f3-e6e5-59bd-9144-f9733223829f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319519} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee8eb52-9b9f-56f1-9ecb-13190d21a579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319819} {:answer/id #uuid "b056fbea-f31f-565e-a6ee-a22a4a856c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319927} {:answer/id #uuid "b321e6e4-fc1e-54d2-9a1a-305a18b80a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax+11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319939} {:answer/id #uuid "b495768c-d594-5351-a058-aad82b451854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=x+11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28a8e3f3-e6e5-59bd-9144-f9733223829f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319519} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5fa981-8f9f-5e72-b3b9-955186ce41e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40530ca3-e238-5640-94b0-1433afa01d34" :question/math-academy-id "q-35310" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{a}+11$, where $a$ is a non-zero constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{a}+11\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y}{a}+11\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{y}{a}+11 \\\\ x-11 & =\\frac{y}{a} \\\\ a(x-11) & =y \\\\ ax-11a & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11a\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185960eb-bfc4-5b04-9b8e-889e9fa563fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402404} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e16aa5-8900-58f3-8772-5eddc1069f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402405} {:answer/id #uuid "de3fec5a-67b7-579c-a809-55a0c1f4c2ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402406} {:answer/id #uuid "10adf48f-3c4e-533f-ba78-f17c436ead07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402407} {:answer/id #uuid "8d0475d5-57f8-5642-b3fc-c1fb1233e0f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7e16aa5-8900-58f3-8772-5eddc1069f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402405} :answer-field/id #uuid "181f4699-3530-5c7f-a1cf-43e09f53ac45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da57a6bc-d0db-59f4-81d7-c54fef1172ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-35379" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}+3).$" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}+3) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\,(\\infty ) \\\\  & =-\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124663 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "349d6fca-cfd5-5899-97f9-619f38ffc399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321547} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6ce2ac-2a25-54b2-839c-b1cc6290b561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322759} {:answer/id #uuid "d539cb73-cc46-54a7-951a-365d9f621a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322807} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c15bef-666c-5440-98b0-35f3376a287f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322920} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb9dd70-fd1d-55b4-8e19-6849ed66d65b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "349d6fca-cfd5-5899-97f9-619f38ffc399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321547} :answer-field/id #uuid "d568f8c0-9a4c-5bd9-a0d2-159cca6bb4c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24c1ddd3-5086-5a99-bc61-14c84e2545e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-35763" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+5}{2{x}^{3}+2x+6}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x+5}{2{x}^{3}+2x+6} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(2+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{2+0+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124664 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bde258b7-1654-5e73-8703-0e42371db8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "544fe8c0-00d7-5582-9b43-a7537bdb2cd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321810} {:answer/id #uuid "8d44af16-7047-5688-8bee-4018f24233a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322274} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f05e28-da65-5e0b-9cd3-41729a024a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322534} {:answer/id #uuid "b324732c-ba06-52ac-9465-f950f027ee85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322547} {:answer/id #uuid "d8824ca2-db5e-5819-9375-3a960341c205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "544fe8c0-00d7-5582-9b43-a7537bdb2cd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321810} :answer-field/id #uuid "1017af27-6db1-5bfd-9bf5-b120516afb73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5a61e3c-7091-5d14-ae25-993daf76ef12" :question/math-academy-id "q-35834" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{12x-3{x}^{4}}{3{x}^{2}+7}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{12x-3{x}^{4}}{3{x}^{2}+7} \\\\  & {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{12x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{12}{x}-3{x}^{2})}{(3+\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{0-3{x}^{2}}{3+0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7739ff43-c68c-58ca-8bbe-3cf898ab85f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322081} {:answer/id #uuid "8d033687-0dda-5d24-a50d-81609e131df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322273} {:answer/id #uuid "8f052679-93df-563a-8c3a-49901beaf19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322283} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2a112f-a59c-5195-8734-7545ee263058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322736} {:answer/id #uuid "eefd406f-e3a9-51c3-9df4-f6d2a447cae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eefd406f-e3a9-51c3-9df4-f6d2a447cae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323031} :answer-field/id #uuid "661e0d23-dac0-58fe-aeb6-82bf0e4b60b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "544516cf-cdec-5de8-af07-1ff7e5be101e" :question/math-academy-id "q-36004" :question/problem "![](images/15/1597ad53471d0d9b44812c5caa31bcb160cfa150d1df9a9314447fa49e62ccff.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in [-6,0]$\n- $x\\in [-6,4)$\n- $x\\in [4,10)$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in [-6,0]$ and $x\\in [4,10).$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/7c/7cb282e3fa66af801ff03816973d5f5f1d0cb41e39d08a8addd27d7aa9c5b9df.png)\n\n      \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $[-6,4]$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/00/000953609a7b3e13ad09473ead62f96aa895c7f94aa618884e69883595004fd4.png)\n\n \n\n Therefore, the correct answer is \"I  and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "014f6668-666f-5040-a144-715105254126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321160} {:answer/id #uuid "26c34079-b0a6-5fc3-9a77-ee93f052ff20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321452} {:answer/id #uuid "80353f56-f611-551a-b4e9-abf19a92d1af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322153} {:answer/id #uuid "968df320-cd19-54ac-b971-d412835fd3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322348} {:answer/id #uuid "f59bf802-b4bc-54f8-8889-7338410f2cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80353f56-f611-551a-b4e9-abf19a92d1af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322153} :answer-field/id #uuid "28b86894-2e1c-5167-ac3e-a4dd5b6514f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d31db013-e3f7-5fe2-aefa-8428da418673" :question/math-academy-id "q-36011" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b02c2e94e144935f0ff96846cd51b5a969fa8f884a7ca1d09abb231b7c945232.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible? \n\n- $x\\in (-4,-2)$\n- $x\\in [-2,3]$\n- $x\\in [-3,3]$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-4,-2)$ and $x\\in [-2,3].$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/53/53fe88780b2ccb7522d2f1baa711162ec99300c2a2d4da29bba9ca532026cd07.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on  the interval $x\\in [-3,3]$  because some lines will  intersect the curve at more than one point.\n\n![](images/63/63cee4fc05ab17a8e69d9bfae578fa83c24d73e4677065a54a33a25e0871e718.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f3bfa4-c874-5502-b110-2b8c72d11d3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321187} {:answer/id #uuid "0676db20-db96-5e83-9dd9-af9bc2d62d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321198} {:answer/id #uuid "89cc5679-849e-548d-a951-2998d3ec013f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322248} {:answer/id #uuid "c04ceba5-0448-5d21-beab-cccea2bd0eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322641} {:answer/id #uuid "ea682fc8-18e7-53c1-b083-08279d59916f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89cc5679-849e-548d-a951-2998d3ec013f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322248} :answer-field/id #uuid "87a0d8da-f7a1-5f82-9cfc-3a0cc85e80a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f36893d-e053-5d18-beeb-2f0d972c9d98" :question/math-academy-id "q-365323" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${4}^{1-2x}\\le 3\\cdot {5}^{2-3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{1-2x} & \\le 3\\cdot {5}^{2-3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{1-2x}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {5}^{2-3x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{1-2x}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{2-3x}) \\\\ (1-2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡4-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ 3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ x(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc794c7-cd0c-5ebc-aa11-9b82e72905fd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits of Piecewise Functions with a Gap in the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6489f85-c078-529a-bb89-256282a9a13d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402497} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee1227f-4f52-5de5-b34e-e560db110256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402498} {:answer/id #uuid "b65d528b-38e9-53d6-b9b7-00b8f1c5ac41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402499} {:answer/id #uuid "e70c9d48-daf4-5c24-8f93-c596a8791141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402500} {:answer/id #uuid "668245ac-4011-59ee-a836-e35d283d1eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b65d528b-38e9-53d6-b9b7-00b8f1c5ac41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402499} :answer-field/id #uuid "9766008d-bd13-5a70-b8fc-147a47588ba7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65911f9a-fa00-54b8-9673-9586456d002e" :question/math-academy-id "q-36696" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The right-sided limit ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ \n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nApproaching $x=0$ from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100977 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0995bfd4-20b1-5f12-9d1d-fcb180a6291e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19ed57d1-1d91-544a-bdae-7940b7113ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321348} {:answer/id #uuid "877e8444-5a27-500f-a477-baed7db698bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322223} {:answer/id #uuid "964d5dce-dd1f-5775-a66c-aba0c11d0274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322343} {:answer/id #uuid "98de86f1-ea9d-579c-b52c-2a809a3accce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322361} {:answer/id #uuid "e14ed661-5e3a-5437-bf1b-eb0a7535207c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e14ed661-5e3a-5437-bf1b-eb0a7535207c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322907} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf487efd-f750-56a9-8fe4-aff8fa623557" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df0978e3-40c7-5749-aacd-3714af936e64" :question/math-academy-id "q-36720" :question/problem "Find the limit at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}6x+1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -4{log}_{2}⁡x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<\\frac{1}{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=6x+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{-}}(6x+1) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow,  approaching from the right, we have $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-4{log}_{2}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}-4{log}_{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-4{log}_{2}⁡\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-4{log}_{2}⁡{2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-4(-1){log}_{2}⁡2 \\\\  & =-4(-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left- and right-sided limits are equal to $4,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100978 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdd4a2e4-b0e6-54e8-972c-3dc194ce4ab3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Do Not Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0331564f-2588-501b-b00b-4bfc21903eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321178} {:answer/id #uuid "26f8feb1-5ef3-5db8-83c3-0253bb71a162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321454} {:answer/id #uuid "30a31cb0-e610-5382-bc68-23ae87a10893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321520} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9a9190-a31e-5666-90db-d2259e210b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322279} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e36c4d-2ff0-5296-a228-00cad37c904e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26f8feb1-5ef3-5db8-83c3-0253bb71a162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321454} :answer-field/id #uuid "6db0b422-9f7f-5b42-ba98-b50b2a2c0d76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2777af2b-a5d2-5c98-9043-4dca19a2df59" :question/math-academy-id "q-36724" :question/problem "Find the limit at $x=3$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi )\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\sqrt[3]{3x-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3\\cdot 3-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi ).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are different, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124472 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22d016a6-ae62-56e2-94ca-cf268596c630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402518} {:answer/id #uuid "07a9ea11-bf6b-5bd3-bc0f-8446bc1bf4cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402519} {:answer/id #uuid "89edd0ab-89db-5aea-aa06-1774d0033938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402520} {:answer/id #uuid "bf173099-427d-522f-a04c-39870e96d661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402521} {:answer/id #uuid "5e473cc0-e97e-5768-bad0-fa0e92282062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89edd0ab-89db-5aea-aa06-1774d0033938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402520} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ab0fae8-fb4f-560c-b176-a3a930105d51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cbc83e7-bc89-5609-b94a-0fe1dfb1bde0" :question/math-academy-id "q-36905" :question/problem "![](images/91/91f073fa77aab423ee33902451e61ad9f033b61b2a875b66ff69708d4361e24f.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\nFrom the above, we see that in each of the four given graphs, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. Therefore, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, none of them correspond to an invertible function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4caea11f-484f-50a4-a520-52df284329ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321753} {:answer/id #uuid "54e75cb9-e5b0-5ea8-9eaf-304e5de715df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321816} {:answer/id #uuid "888f7548-fc8c-5732-aae9-9e3aa70555ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322236} {:answer/id #uuid "c304d552-6f05-5067-a107-0c7b855a1c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322661} {:answer/id #uuid "f99f1c2f-bc18-5796-a3e2-28f9f9b5b60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "888f7548-fc8c-5732-aae9-9e3aa70555ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322236} :answer-field/id #uuid "9392c5de-5972-51d1-8356-6d7fe3ba1f53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e891cfb3-f978-5b44-b8bf-4d4f9f71b503" :question/math-academy-id "q-36941" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3cbdac583a4cdf1b5eb761892934516a0b4edd1487bd402ae0014859c4a0ad53.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-2,0]$\n- $x\\in (-2,2]$\n- $x\\in [-4,-2]\\cup [2,4]$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-2,0].$ Indeed, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/d7/d7c6e8d314483ad67e89ce5d4675b7af27fa08c88a4ed9d95eed9ad2a4da51cc.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-2,2]$ and $x\\in [-4,-2]\\cup [2,4]$ because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/ce/cee829f7e3d5f32b0acb54cb5c40406d8391d2a2967c68df0c1f5e1cae87d729.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "231604dc-fe8f-59be-90fe-37af7a1bb648" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has no Vertical Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15f2dcf3-b7da-5207-a75c-12f5aab228de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321324} {:answer/id #uuid "2d333293-812e-550f-be94-d57de33d86b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321498} {:answer/id #uuid "3e848eef-3137-5776-aaca-65af02d8767e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321638} {:answer/id #uuid "40ee8b23-a651-5c65-b7f7-c17eaad7aa2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321664} {:answer/id #uuid "77dc533c-b6a2-5d5c-aab6-a0419fb093db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40ee8b23-a651-5c65-b7f7-c17eaad7aa2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321664} :answer-field/id #uuid "7758aae7-f886-5729-92ed-f1adeb80d088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07860f00-c712-5f63-bfe2-a24e46e0db46" :question/math-academy-id "q-37070" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-3x}{8x-6}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{2}-3x}{8x-6} & =\\frac{x(4x-3)}{2(4x-3)}=\\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\n![](images/3e/3e6ebc8eaec86276442b13fde934234fdaf9c4a457398bb1b792c6910350e44c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100978 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdd4a2e4-b0e6-54e8-972c-3dc194ce4ab3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Do Not Coincide"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f56bdd5-a607-5af2-b88c-b12454f5dbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392103} {:answer/id #uuid "f80ff926-3aa7-5c1c-b968-b56b246178c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392104} {:answer/id #uuid "544549f0-b759-5c27-aa3c-4223a9df60df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392105} {:answer/id #uuid "4fccaa0b-2b3d-58c3-881e-5c696c14c18c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392106} {:answer/id #uuid "8a29317f-e4cb-5886-99c5-3539d5b2e238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a29317f-e4cb-5886-99c5-3539d5b2e238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392107} :answer-field/id #uuid "04efa2b5-a248-5966-8fd4-c3f5d9dc1a1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14d10373-1baf-5303-9f9f-7dbe595ac72a" :question/math-academy-id "q-37488" :question/problem "Find the left-sided limit at $x=0$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x\\le 0 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124661 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32cbf366-20bd-5a52-a14f-83add698bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Rational Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "400f0091-175f-5587-84b8-9c92e0445d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321651} {:answer/id #uuid "463439b5-0cfc-525b-89ed-52c822437a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321701} {:answer/id #uuid "689d0a5e-3cbe-5251-93c7-9224aa737768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Does not exist." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321963} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbe2c23-cb67-5e33-ae6d-6470a9763727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323149} {:answer/id #uuid "feda0822-7e47-547f-b9e2-686fb2b48328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "463439b5-0cfc-525b-89ed-52c822437a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321701} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7b2c792-031f-57b9-a80b-617c4464ff0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be3f2ca8-5451-5c14-b7e4-a6be8ff17159" :question/math-academy-id "q-3762" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6{x}^{3}-9}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6{x}^{3}-9} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-2+\\frac{1}{x})}{(6-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124515 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd0537f8-b091-580a-974e-8e928d54939d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vector Under a Linear Transformation Given its Standard Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ef73df9-84ec-5f34-a71b-fe726195bfd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321383} {:answer/id #uuid "3efa9ca2-24ec-5607-8469-50498346ff15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321642} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9025d4-18ab-5385-94bd-26181314bc40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322992} {:answer/id #uuid "eedbea08-593d-5c3a-a72c-6b6885d23514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323029} {:answer/id #uuid "fa65dcba-2e33-5614-8e1f-2c508af52fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3efa9ca2-24ec-5607-8469-50498346ff15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321642} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba434382-4384-5494-9593-15482668b874" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12f3bb3a-f83c-5bc7-89c6-1af5031ec24d" :question/math-academy-id "q-37881" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $T$ and the vector $v$ given below. If $T$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T,$ find $T(v).$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that $\\,T(v)=T\\cdot v,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =T\\cdot v \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91fbca89-c577-5e53-936f-3be4371d6739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322305} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d2dab8-cede-584b-9e20-5115b3d14dfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322441} {:answer/id #uuid "e53eb49d-9767-5e28-a2f3-ab6cd8a7a0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322942} {:answer/id #uuid "ed71d143-9343-52d1-af9f-9b40f8a55525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323019} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9ee2db-b77f-5cab-9284-b745cebd2507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4d2dab8-cede-584b-9e20-5115b3d14dfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322441} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bfbccbb-cbf5-52c1-a3ca-3a74ad96eac6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb646a61-3e18-55e1-850b-c5318af9cd57" :question/math-academy-id "q-37900" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$ and $f(-1)=-3,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=f(-1)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8126b5-2054-5d88-865f-be6cbe674d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321368} {:answer/id #uuid "3466518b-1130-594a-9f91-8439ec16fa46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} {:answer/id #uuid "51eee8c3-23ea-5613-a452-4c2182222cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321794} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba4b8d7-052a-5cf2-86db-50636fe22d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321871} {:answer/id #uuid "c2131ada-4ea2-5f29-85f1-aa42dc442a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3466518b-1130-594a-9f91-8439ec16fa46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} :answer-field/id #uuid "617b107f-dc16-5899-aab5-ab0f5850cc8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9112180a-ac6b-5472-ba8b-819d346ead1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37915" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c36dbce847c719accc77fa15f33dfb9a87cfae669691afe30f7693663076d2e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/52/52fe94bb303a001e89f9db221e9730d4ca8c5c284217aa20fb6084ee041dec66.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{10}{16}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{20}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "979859e4-b60c-548d-8da6-b180400e1b07" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11f2d595-4c96-599f-b04a-ef1a4facbf7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324134} {:answer/id #uuid "35476165-d1db-5ee5-b183-b3201978c25b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324408} {:answer/id #uuid "36554c2e-a686-5080-983a-7fd2be339db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324414} {:answer/id #uuid "5dc9de58-8130-508b-ad5a-dc48cfe4914d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324751} {:answer/id #uuid "a45499fc-34c3-5b71-b300-396e74e38e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11f2d595-4c96-599f-b04a-ef1a4facbf7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324134} :answer-field/id #uuid "17302b73-c3c5-5ca7-9ed6-b18a09cd2b48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "687c1640-263b-54f0-8cd7-1fc76a39da2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38180" :question/problem "Find the value of $|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|-(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0+1(0-6)+3(-2-0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-6)+3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-6-6 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "874fff2e-ad05-545d-b9da-54afafb38522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dD}{dt} = kD, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127310} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbdf968-a3aa-5c7c-bee0-f3e5cdef2d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dD}{dt} =-kt, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127311} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4bda65-3235-5e5f-bca3-363e844b6c69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dD}{dt} = kt, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127312} {:answer/id #uuid "1d0e73de-fef3-542f-948e-ab7d50987655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dD}{dt} =-\\frac{k}{t^{2}}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127313} {:answer/id #uuid "77b6d1c9-13bd-55b9-bf57-aaf485a87dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dD}{dt} = kt^{2}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127314}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce1a994-8031-5aae-8482-b5c5180c0212" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127309} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c5a7d28-429d-5869-a68d-4d92f36195cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=kt,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402624} {:answer/id #uuid "c87a8f69-4b14-5096-8f83-ef9eed495b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402625} {:answer/id #uuid "ed230839-dcea-57e5-a62d-204f0b996646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402626} {:answer/id #uuid "7b35292d-237a-56fe-bfb7-1ff65aaee600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=kD,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402627} {:answer/id #uuid "78931a87-f659-54d0-8ce9-c7e8a7e38fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-kt,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c87a8f69-4b14-5096-8f83-ef9eed495b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402625} :answer-field/id #uuid "a733e896-0f87-5db4-b6c7-ab7a478c7bf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f8683314-e1cb-5e62-aca4-60ca1e1c5bf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-38313" :question/problem "A skater is skating straight down a ramp. The displacement $D$ of the skater increases at a rate that's proportional to the square of the time $t,$ in seconds.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of displacement $D$ at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time since he started skating?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*A skater is skating straight down a ramp. The **displacement** $D$ of the skater **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t,$ in seconds. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *displacement* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *square of the time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}∝{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the displacement *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "374ae646-8b4e-5b0e-bd7e-4acb5d1c0ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324420} {:answer/id #uuid "7d1612fc-e261-50ce-9313-fe26f50738e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325026} {:answer/id #uuid "c86a33ca-8732-5643-a35e-d2538c0e58e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325709} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e6cb1a-5418-5746-8b18-030e1c8789a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325813} {:answer/id #uuid "f0206e22-2c8b-5150-92b8-e52b0d52a598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "374ae646-8b4e-5b0e-bd7e-4acb5d1c0ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324420} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b0c2133-1c76-5099-a8ab-82d13698a8d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aa66e43c-92cc-5831-9942-3be250ef9ed8" :question/math-academy-id "q-38546" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fdb835ed2998fae4d8afc3fd5e593bac6c72b1ee45c8b4fe01bdc4348322d1e.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$\n- The function is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34aa8f4e-20f1-5dda-ac53-6eb411543897" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09148cf6-e660-5422-9fa7-a9f4738defd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402651} {:answer/id #uuid "3340c634-8f83-5193-ae75-2b37a7b6c946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402652} {:answer/id #uuid "f4481476-873e-5b4e-a202-210e8449c587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402653} {:answer/id #uuid "63b1b59c-8954-5973-ae03-cc16a86d080d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402654} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f0241d-3539-5ced-b138-fdde689dc4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3340c634-8f83-5193-ae75-2b37a7b6c946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402652} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f13134e-6c13-54bc-aaf2-8a968a13df74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4ced118-ede6-56c5-a59a-002b28515d4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38580" :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea87f040e4249a04c21dd6c8466ffd194d60753056c29f00d8956e182db61ff3.png)\n\nThe graph of the polynomial $y=F(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The degree of $F(x)$ is odd.\nII. The degree of $F(x)$ is even.\nIII. The coefficient of the leading term is positive." :question/worked-solution "The end behavior of $F(x)$ is\n\n- $F(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ and\n\n- $F(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $F(x)$ is odd, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $F(x)$ is positive.\n\nSo II is false, and I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129119 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "117b0f2a-a546-5344-bfb8-3e0c41afbbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324131} {:answer/id #uuid "395db5bb-f296-567c-91ea-db87d69ea219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324435} {:answer/id #uuid "41eb7615-c4fd-5775-8878-99d3a9cb550f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k+V,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324504} {:answer/id #uuid "b71b1c89-8e55-546e-8485-90db0a36d0ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-V" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325544} {:answer/id #uuid "f047554c-a8e1-5e18-881a-83ed20299fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-tV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "117b0f2a-a546-5344-bfb8-3e0c41afbbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324131} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdf79fb1-73e0-5037-8b11-7d7606258ed9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eafaeb15-26b6-5234-a17e-a3acc08c3e0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-38593" :question/problem "The number of weekly sales of a particular book decreases with time. The number of units sold at any given moment decreases at a rate that's proportional to the number of units sold at that moment. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of units sold $V(t)$ at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time since the book first went on sale?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **number of units sold** at any given moment **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to **the number of units sold** at that moment.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *number of units sold* of a particular book varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *number of units sold.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝V.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number of units sold *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34aa8f4e-20f1-5dda-ac53-6eb411543897" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "520ce037-6d65-5a87-816c-38b9a9d22f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324642} {:answer/id #uuid "b137372b-d54b-51cb-8c70-5c8eaabe8e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325482} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ee0489-97b3-566f-8f0e-c62d506da27a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325704} {:answer/id #uuid "cb11e15c-7b44-5232-8e56-3e85080ab86c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325731} {:answer/id #uuid "e61483f3-87ce-5ef0-b035-b6a704d798b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b137372b-d54b-51cb-8c70-5c8eaabe8e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325482} :answer-field/id #uuid "ceb549ca-f772-54ab-ac92-58fb214b0833" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f40578d-2e88-5fde-bb73-48381fd621fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-38617" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb4959f437e11b767be136a2a024260d45ed29805cd57b1832d74d7cc2da11c1.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $y=P(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n- The degree of $P(x)$ is odd.\n- The coefficient of the leading term is negative." :question/worked-solution "The end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129144 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7cf112-ab08-5e7a-855d-91bb69ec7f76" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polynomial Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fbb6282-911b-5b66-b192-305d440d6e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x^{6} + x^{4} - 5x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127562} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a044cc-7963-5872-b7e6-6d9a2f224fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-7x^{8} + 6x^{7} - 4x^{3} + 2x^{2} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127563} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb2454f-bf3e-5aa4-afce-9896ef787f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-5x^{5} + 3x^{2} + x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127564} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5b9971-58f5-5d6d-9f95-7c155519b2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-x^{3} + 2x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127565} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0c079c-6769-57c1-b736-a89c4eef2463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{3} - x^{2} + x - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127566}] :answer-field/id #uuid "db6524ac-b4e5-521e-a137-1133e610aa7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127561} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb2ff6c4-35ee-5c86-88b6-f2b778bb9b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{3}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402657} {:answer/id #uuid "0cf46694-f517-57c1-b656-f30bd243fe5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-7{x}^{8}+6{x}^{7}-4{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402658} {:answer/id #uuid "0cabba40-fb5e-5284-8447-f1cf64e6820e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{6}+{x}^{4}-5x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402659} {:answer/id #uuid "e52d814a-f821-5e6e-934c-6f5a0f7ba906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402660} {:answer/id #uuid "343e1b78-b8cf-5288-841e-bb541f7dedd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5{x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}+x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb2ff6c4-35ee-5c86-88b6-f2b778bb9b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{3}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402657} :answer-field/id #uuid "702788a0-aadc-5877-8e1c-f586a9038e3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e1b36cf-56ef-56e7-80a1-3a4e3d34ad42" :question/math-academy-id "q-38624" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d873bd2af8e397d6c540267340b6105f413928c4279b939110aa4bb5b00d8948.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials could be the function shown in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is odd, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\n- the $y$-intercept is negative, which means that $P(0)<0.$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, only $P(x)=-{x}^{3}+2x-1$ satisties all these conditions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129144 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7cf112-ab08-5e7a-855d-91bb69ec7f76" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polynomial Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ec8bba-61a9-5e53-9f83-ae6306a35d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{x}^{6}+\\sqrt{7}{x}^{3}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324133} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3e9e3c-8f63-5b55-a2b5-d6ae2f7cc85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324219} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6cc728-1b58-586c-b553-5f4c6de4cade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3{x}^{3}+8x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324575} {:answer/id #uuid "858b3ba7-0126-52d6-a92c-623111bcf464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{x}^{8}+4{x}^{2}+6x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325099} {:answer/id #uuid "d96579c4-211b-512e-819d-f2e5755afaf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6{x}^{5}+4{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d3e9e3c-8f63-5b55-a2b5-d6ae2f7cc85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324219} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c01c0f4-68e1-5f41-ac69-b50a572974f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c04700c1-06e0-50a6-8674-83b9d2f36915" :question/math-academy-id "q-38626" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb467eed4327c7feaa4cb0aad4495032fe52518891bb6d33227352c953ce4d35.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials could be the function shown in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\n- the $y$-intercept is positive, which means that $P(0)>0.$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the only one that satisfies all three criteria is $y=-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-x+2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42f76b4e-c2e3-5210-b8b9-db6bc53bafc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324510} {:answer/id #uuid "868d64bf-05a4-5a8a-bb2f-f215d1a3fb4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325114} {:answer/id #uuid "aefcef1a-2486-5ad4-889b-d4796ad4702f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325458} {:answer/id #uuid "dfae7751-a48a-56fb-8fe9-dabe433a0205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325924} {:answer/id #uuid "fd71fe36-ab9d-5173-8340-e63507c5a1eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "868d64bf-05a4-5a8a-bb2f-f215d1a3fb4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325114} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1d289fa-530e-56e8-81da-88ee4282c3b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbe22a70-053f-5354-8d7b-dc32dacb2db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-38770" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd19717bd41b787346a96394ce3bc4e38fe690340bb73bd3d11139d41ff977e2.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=2$ is a simple root.\n- $x=-1$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n- $p(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=2$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=-1$  has an even multiplicity because at $x=-1$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ moves right in the positive direction towards $+\\infty ,$ the $y$ value moves up in the positive direction towards $+\\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129094 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eac988ba-ea97-52c7-90a7-d451ee7f49e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Rational Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2ecaae7-3c35-55c3-949a-8bc3ad796eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325506} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc63a98-85e5-5224-9b6a-a9c5d63399a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325592} {:answer/id #uuid "c44ce678-da9d-5026-bdf2-d1c31648eeec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325659} {:answer/id #uuid "cc959c14-7321-5ed8-8a0d-e560f10b3ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325745} {:answer/id #uuid "fb645e78-49d9-5243-b606-2ce3f8529efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,7)\\cup (7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2ecaae7-3c35-55c3-949a-8bc3ad796eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325506} :answer-field/id #uuid "aebe9ce5-b166-55c6-9070-84eeedf233a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90ecbfdb-7e05-5892-b0cb-107f95150e27" :question/math-academy-id "q-38831" :question/problem "Determine the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-{x}^{2}}{7x}$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-{x}^{2}}{7x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(1-x)}{7x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-x}{7},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $x\\ne 0.$\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded point $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,0),$ and $(0,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129302 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "503a2d0a-ba6c-5529-9f26-b83c769a26a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b52373-27a9-501e-8947-0c1f244a213e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324149} {:answer/id #uuid "5bec4237-30c8-54eb-a9f5-13ccaf205551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324732} {:answer/id #uuid "85ed53c0-2287-5169-b4e2-c59609989228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325105} {:answer/id #uuid "d964678d-9ceb-5d76-929a-65b4dd6a6052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325861} {:answer/id #uuid "df8ff16b-fe1f-5c14-b271-df5bace274b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df8ff16b-fe1f-5c14-b271-df5bace274b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325923} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9cd61a3-c73b-5511-815b-f6d529fa4ad2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45b870b0-b774-5b07-bef2-72f771e77f18" :question/math-academy-id "q-38999" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $y=\\frac{2}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "135f42a4-d1ee-5f2c-af3a-0c8aa0176bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324143} {:answer/id #uuid "268df909-c8fb-563f-af2f-c08134584f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324283} {:answer/id #uuid "69e770f2-5fac-5581-b92a-b1b948231e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324857} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3c6363-b72d-5a0d-9eec-918fa9888a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325152} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9d3f67-6d9d-5067-8456-4fe529f4d545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69e770f2-5fac-5581-b92a-b1b948231e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324857} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f2953d1-fb0d-5e3e-8ec9-21bd1672c52d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f572c08e-edf8-5f9d-9827-c106bc98ece4" :question/math-academy-id "q-39033" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}-15x-25$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-25$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-25}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 5,\\,\\pm 25,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{25}{3}\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ are $-1$ and $-\\frac{5}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $3$ | $5$ | $-15$ | $-25$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $25$ |\n|  | $3$ | $0$ | $-15$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})({x}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})(x+\\sqrt{5})(x-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{5}{3},-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{3}-\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{5}=-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d1d0905-b59d-5d4a-909c-cea1b665a291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402677} {:answer/id #uuid "f2fa7f39-114c-5150-972f-6a6f4f47133a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402678} {:answer/id #uuid "77d63650-bd84-5a65-ad3f-110fff12b852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402679} {:answer/id #uuid "a04b8362-fdb8-5b5f-84e2-c7356b9ecb6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402680} {:answer/id #uuid "6d67d68d-f930-5fbf-a8ad-1dfef015cd8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d1d0905-b59d-5d4a-909c-cea1b665a291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402677} :answer-field/id #uuid "570ae7f1-bab1-51b3-a682-ac5497a6d1ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "494506ed-3be9-5fa5-a304-e992bf8e5303" :question/math-academy-id "q-3946" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{2}-5x+16).$" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{2}-5x+16) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129096 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ad95b4e-e367-5e26-800c-1a87a537ca83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Trigonometric Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "433bf40e-b58f-5f73-acd3-043724f4f0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324514} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d08469-ad7b-5338-83f8-aa4eda61a77a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325534} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4f7279-6b09-5d5a-b7dd-914bf38083f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325571} {:answer/id #uuid "eab55c63-7bc8-5884-94c9-5575e81cf09c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326040} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a36cbf-0b55-59cd-a76e-8d0bcaae5e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "433bf40e-b58f-5f73-acd3-043724f4f0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324514} :answer-field/id #uuid "c279fba3-e32a-5884-932a-62cb2020e6bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bdf79360-c3a2-5f70-b20e-0e9fca989978" :question/math-academy-id "q-39765" :question/problem "Find the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-1})$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "The function is a composition of two functions, a rational function, and a sine function.\n\nThe sine is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the rational function.\n\nLikewise, the rational function $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-1}$ is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(x-1)(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded points $x=-1$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the domain our original function consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1),$ $(-1,1),$ and $(1,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129096 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ad95b4e-e367-5e26-800c-1a87a537ca83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Trigonometric Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00cc317f-9860-521f-8e35-82b0b96775d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323979} {:answer/id #uuid "1336a17d-6837-5de5-bf71-47722d0cc700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324141} {:answer/id #uuid "20176902-9b53-52f9-8032-b69446a5fc1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324236} {:answer/id #uuid "a203df96-a113-582e-930a-799f5e42f2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325346} {:answer/id #uuid "d6617b71-5ede-536c-a0f7-693baf883f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20176902-9b53-52f9-8032-b69446a5fc1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324236} :answer-field/id #uuid "0394152c-8a6f-59be-83ce-2e7929fa5c84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ae959f9-6d60-5b89-84b8-0851a7c0c42d" :question/math-academy-id "q-39769" :question/problem "Find the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "The function is a composition of two functions, a radical function, and a cosine function.\n\nThe cosine is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the radical function.\n\nLikewise, the radical function $\\sqrt{2x+1}$ is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,2x\\ge -1\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ is $[-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )$, and the function is continuous on this interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce1c8713-2cf3-5d3e-be72-a3135dfaed78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1,\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402690} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5c07fd-aef8-50a6-bb7a-0214e75d1fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=1,\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402691} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d397dd-0706-56c3-a545-4f1e11b5d1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402692} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3acbb2-152b-5386-a5b3-d4d5f9778946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402693} {:answer/id #uuid "466aa8d5-5559-5bc5-8a5d-ddd22b7bb453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9d397dd-0706-56c3-a545-4f1e11b5d1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402692} :answer-field/id #uuid "aba4d313-1d11-58c4-bb5b-3d3a12220a0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca4d349e-c1ee-5a19-a1e0-b0c9e7e02dd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-39793" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x}{(x+3)(x+1)(x-1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)(x+1)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the three solutions $x=-3,$ $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are  $x=-3,\\,$ $x=-1$ and $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a602f803-9ee4-55ae-ba4f-d88ab6ec6a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402697} {:answer/id #uuid "d1acf715-1373-5a06-b01b-ab3b303c535d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402698} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbc63c9-13bb-57db-8074-636542eaac49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402699} {:answer/id #uuid "66e7f76d-26a7-52c7-993f-99c8712a83c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402700} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad6dfaf-76c0-5356-9336-dc60a8e77b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ad6dfaf-76c0-5356-9336-dc60a8e77b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402701} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e77536-5db4-551c-9973-483a9864ffa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "10e486fb-a153-567a-b122-83eab72817c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-39936" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{4}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{({x}^{2}-1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{(x-1)(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}}{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd0aa5fc-79de-525e-a688-a8a2d2fcf858" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Constant in a Transformed Reciprocal Function Given the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36817ff5-94b5-5e0c-aa6e-e4adfd7b868f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324418} {:answer/id #uuid "58722e71-13e2-5d86-b77b-8b1c37339129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324706} {:answer/id #uuid "796d99a4-c609-5611-b9b4-030174fd4671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324984} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb00e44-4f46-5766-841d-29d6d2a9f5b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325767} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1f23f3-9644-55d1-99f6-e9850f0217bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ceb00e44-4f46-5766-841d-29d6d2a9f5b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325767} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a286a71-fa21-5505-b96c-f7267c23eddf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d984b68-ba41-5abd-a3f2-afc368e8283a" :question/math-academy-id "q-40027" :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4}{3x+b}$ is $(-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (-4,\\infty ).$ Find the value of $b.$" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+b & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-b \\\\ x & =-\\frac{b}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=-\\frac{b}{3}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=-4$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $-\\frac{b}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{b}{3} & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{b}{3} & =4 \\\\ b & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129096 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ad95b4e-e367-5e26-800c-1a87a537ca83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Trigonometric Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3497a718-c1a6-51e6-af1a-953f981abd22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\pi ,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324397} {:answer/id #uuid "40780318-a745-5f1a-8bba-affb853af852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-\\pi ,0)\\cup (0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324489} {:answer/id #uuid "7216b300-5c68-553d-ab9a-ac506700f72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-\\pi ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324916} {:answer/id #uuid "a0aeec49-d531-50db-bf21-b8578d86c37d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325333} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d962bb-8ba4-5e15-a962-658461433d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\pi ,0)\\cup (0,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5d962bb-8ba4-5e15-a962-658461433d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\pi ,0)\\cup (0,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325832} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f7faea1-42ca-5601-a0d5-7ae190223c12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14982c8b-d480-564b-82c7-d131ac68638d" :question/math-academy-id "q-40081" :question/problem "The interval $I$ consists of all real numbers $x$ such that $-\\pi \\le x\\le \\pi .$ What is the largest subset of $I$ on which the function $g(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ is continuous?" :question/worked-solution "Trigonometric functions are continuous at all points in their respective domains.\n\nThe function $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is continuous over the entire interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ but the function $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=-\\pi ,0,\\pi .$ \n\nTherefore, the domain of $g(x)$ consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\pi ,0)$ and $(0,\\pi ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\pi ,0)\\cup (0,\\pi )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326831 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c80b75e7-19b2-534b-97f8-aba563ebe058" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Cubic Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b2e8b63-81e7-5a1c-a343-d2e83bac41de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324201} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4cf899-ff99-50a7-ab03-977e0a939e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324597} {:answer/id #uuid "849305c9-d929-518e-bf23-35902ab0355f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325088} {:answer/id #uuid "be244597-d382-5777-9a00-d13314a385f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (\\frac{1}{2},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325604} {:answer/id #uuid "fa451380-973c-50c6-af68-c706839c4054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "849305c9-d929-518e-bf23-35902ab0355f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325088} :answer-field/id #uuid "2359e231-cc4e-518b-8b28-8c3c01996302" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f08a6c50-6d74-51fe-a849-876f9c4eebbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-40347" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $(x+2)(2x-1)(x-3)>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)(2x-1)(x-3).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=3.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-2,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (3,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4034e61-63dc-5f72-8275-e9c81290767d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Polar Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "287b66ce-eea9-5761-8ae3-e433ccc705bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324296} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ad43de-68fe-5a9c-aea2-663552beff50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325393} {:answer/id #uuid "b8aea7da-f08f-5e62-9eb6-3d6d90b0ef32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325556} {:answer/id #uuid "c1392b6e-aa07-545b-9154-c8ff037361cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325631} {:answer/id #uuid "c4dd1117-4024-55d3-ab48-87b95cec4c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4dd1117-4024-55d3-ab48-87b95cec4c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325666} :answer-field/id #uuid "733270aa-9c3b-5f1e-a50b-48efd158ae2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ebf40886-fb73-5481-a019-3b75d93ee8ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-40791" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d5c95dca34605acc8f66ca24f11e8d048ed3490ae859c55e7f5eb42806307071.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the curve shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is a circle of radius $6$ centered at the origin.\n\nTherefore, its polar equation is $r=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092219 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given Some Information About Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1de63e38-80fb-54c4-9a7c-5a96600b508f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},4\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324222} {:answer/id #uuid "23d41aa2-c3a0-5270-abba-3a1f3207c3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324266} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4dd8b9-9d94-588f-a6d0-7d91017750d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324340} {:answer/id #uuid "81916b05-53d5-5cfe-b7c6-b0642b2c06ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8\\sqrt{3},8\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325056} {:answer/id #uuid "82355246-8fcd-5520-a5e5-83b3cb9b05c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8\\sqrt{2},8\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23d41aa2-c3a0-5270-abba-3a1f3207c3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324266} :answer-field/id #uuid "3036c726-f5d4-51ab-a7e4-77d619c6072b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "417c4b2b-c91e-563b-b92c-41495ea7b2b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-40826" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(8,\\theta +\\pi ).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(8,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+\\pi )=(8,\\frac{9\\pi }{4})=(8,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "407616a9-e448-5652-9882-a1edf19600f8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Radial Line Given Its Polar Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "558d39e0-0ab6-5d33-b3a0-396e26bb8653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/aa/aa72b03a85b16e3a9ef9dc3f65f4126cdee0339ac722fcfe8a1f21973796f623.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392384} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd87d8b-270f-573d-9412-5025cd44dba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/10/10d1cdb8ecbfe7f7153618b5da57fecefbf8711d395cf3c6f5c879f3169977f9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392385} {:answer/id #uuid "97873c7d-0308-59b1-889c-345b9fb3b8f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/80/802bb767b28c38598663d928d66338086adf08b80bc5887a7795704183ed2212.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392386} {:answer/id #uuid "bee250d6-c6dd-5d05-9b3d-34683cc111c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/85/85271401b413b162990a99e905ebecbfb4048953e77bcb5808c0b729c1fc7de3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392387} {:answer/id #uuid "521ffe4f-8b8b-5b4e-afbd-79c02578ee0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2b/2b88d001cbc59e0c46922b6e369644e50a02eff26c800e9d571147988357918e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97873c7d-0308-59b1-889c-345b9fb3b8f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/80/802bb767b28c38598663d928d66338086adf08b80bc5887a7795704183ed2212.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392386} :answer-field/id #uuid "93203f91-1d26-5d83-a83e-32f479d20351" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15ae4fee-42a8-584b-a59a-68427466f591" :question/math-academy-id "q-40828" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve  $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{10}?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{10}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\frac{11\\pi }{10}$ with the positive $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, the angle it makes with the negative $x$-axis is \n\n$$\n\\frac{11\\pi }{10}-\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{10}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe correct answer is shown below.\n\n![](images/80/802bb767b28c38598663d928d66338086adf08b80bc5887a7795704183ed2212.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129102 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd6703d2-36f6-5665-90e3-f75aea5f663b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Expression for the Domain of a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56259cef-6878-51cd-b1f5-eeaf49702140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324688} {:answer/id #uuid "654e271d-49cd-5e3c-9c7d-b37514a25a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324810} {:answer/id #uuid "8b458fe6-2549-577d-823d-3fcf10021ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325153} {:answer/id #uuid "d136e134-3728-57dd-b18c-c29e658d49ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325789} {:answer/id #uuid "fd810710-4cf3-54c8-88a7-9403d3f91aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{n\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "654e271d-49cd-5e3c-9c7d-b37514a25a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324810} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff7a4b9e-8f3f-5b3c-98a8-33205f758cf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a89fb0a0-3a36-552f-b729-6c5581cebcd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-41106" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})+1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})+1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 3x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ 3x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3\\cdot 3}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115202 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc0be03-9369-508e-9765-8c88871ec20b" :knowledge-point/title "The Range of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87c6f1e6-0d33-5cdc-af67-5f19a9b4678a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325127} {:answer/id #uuid "cb12b821-bce4-53ba-809b-8073091be039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325732} {:answer/id #uuid "cf3e7d3c-28cb-551b-814d-24c71777a04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325772} {:answer/id #uuid "dbdb2d32-06a5-584c-b837-e088b83a23f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325886} {:answer/id #uuid "eb32a561-e406-5ef8-b8f6-32579968df6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87c6f1e6-0d33-5cdc-af67-5f19a9b4678a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325127} :answer-field/id #uuid "14aade30-4dcf-54fe-90ad-21a8d183cf5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d356ad64-d32b-5d91-870e-d983fd54ca81" :question/math-academy-id "q-41240" :question/problem "Determine the range of the function $f(x)=2{log}_{5}⁡(2x-1)+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of the function $y={log}_{5}⁡(x)$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Point of Intersection of a Circle and a Radial Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dd96ba1-2b4a-52b0-ae56-d48afc1ad438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},4\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324095} {:answer/id #uuid "2ed05277-6e18-5e15-8940-2ac1cc7e711a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324343} {:answer/id #uuid "45a4f7ba-02b2-575e-9878-19dffb79d143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,4\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324534} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba9dd42-5731-5299-842c-9b4b8a43aecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324727} {:answer/id #uuid "eedf37c4-e836-577f-8644-c79747d63f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eedf37c4-e836-577f-8644-c79747d63f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326080} :answer-field/id #uuid "c09b660e-acc3-5ec3-9d23-f8cb222700d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7c864f9-8a06-59dd-91e8-789300497ddc" :question/math-academy-id "q-41263" :question/problem "![](images/34/34b473c8e212297d8cb434fe0a2dd9872cfa2fda924ac3a4d528bc1318af8c5f.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=8$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(8,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-4\\sqrt{3},4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8058df30-04de-555c-adfa-61fbf2b0ff31" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions in One Variable With Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15017b89-6a83-59c3-8ca4-1e65d3e32c85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{1-2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324161} {:answer/id #uuid "3a903b7b-dcca-5c4f-8920-9a1546e6dcf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-14y+45}{5(1-2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324443} {:answer/id #uuid "c79526ea-db45-5cfa-a2b6-882255022a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14y}{5(1-2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325697} {:answer/id #uuid "dc042986-d3e3-5167-8273-d4f156249300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26y-45}{5(1-2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325890} {:answer/id #uuid "e379b819-7fe0-521e-bbc1-1371d8e50ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y+9}{1-2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a903b7b-dcca-5c4f-8920-9a1546e6dcf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-14y+45}{5(1-2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324443} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e51594-6076-558b-b4c1-486d8c2bf0d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e010d20d-afd2-59fa-8a1c-50d451ce4776" :question/math-academy-id "q-41576" :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}=p$ and $\\frac{9-4y}{1-2y}=q,$ what is $p+q?$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to add the following fractions:\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}+\\frac{9-4y}{1-2y}\n$$\n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $5(1-2y)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}+\\frac{9-4y}{1-2y} & = \\\\ \\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}+\\frac{5}{5}\\cdot \\frac{9-4y}{1-2y} & = \\\\ \\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}+\\frac{5(9-4y)}{5(1-2y)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6y+5(9-4y)}{5(1-2y)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6y+45-20y}{5(1-2y)} & = \\\\ \\frac{-14y+45}{5(1-2y)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129124 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5696c2d5-4e35-5ed3-a4a2-6fe9b158e461" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d1c4191-fab1-56ef-9a9e-5df4f5a37573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [4,6]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324871} {:answer/id #uuid "705447cb-ac85-5790-aadf-544a4a2ca55d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [-2,6]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324896} {:answer/id #uuid "c37b50d3-93b0-5e4b-a2f6-ad5ca8b620b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [3,6]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325652} {:answer/id #uuid "d9fbb5f9-1876-576d-956d-164f993762da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [2,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325870} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d1017a-3133-56bf-ad84-e9bd0f77a8d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [2,6]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3d1017a-3133-56bf-ad84-e9bd0f77a8d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [2,6]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325976} :answer-field/id #uuid "5600e685-5275-5ef3-a75f-13f121192c83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8be6063d-ee1d-5aea-9fe3-f1f6572dff56" :question/math-academy-id "q-41840" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})+4?$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-2$ and then adding $4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})\\le 1 \\\\ 2 & \\ge -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})\\ge -2 \\\\ 6 & \\ge -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})+4\\ge 2 \\\\ 2 & \\le y\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y\\in [2,6].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89362140-1a17-56fd-a9ab-4da336112bee" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column or Row of the Identity Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a95370d-414b-5eac-8919-246de33eac09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2\\sqrt{5} & -2.5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099604} {:answer/id #uuid "a84bcb4e-521f-5ea1-9075-3e249dbed932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3.5 \\\\ -2.5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099605} {:answer/id #uuid "e006e13f-10ad-5b98-98da-0e4073b443c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099606} {:answer/id #uuid "2c32ad2d-131b-5f58-b76f-c00ad96820b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{7} \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099607} {:answer/id #uuid "695edc9d-dd29-5f2a-b8fd-06ab5abd1f2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{7} & -3 & 3.5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099608}] :answer-field/id #uuid "954dd7be-0875-59ee-8cb2-ab4ad7d267c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099603} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e77b0e35-b8ea-5618-ab6c-8503315df0e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3.5 \\\\ -2.5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392443} {:answer/id #uuid "b90bbe8b-d8e5-5080-9aa2-4142743a4b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7} \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392444} {:answer/id #uuid "6b52aa96-4f70-58ea-b2cf-28f779b7b43b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2\\sqrt{5} & -2.5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392445} {:answer/id #uuid "d06d67b6-8acb-58a8-957c-d55d012736a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392446} {:answer/id #uuid "e47cb90f-8189-59b3-96b0-469513eb54fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7} & -3 & 3.5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b90bbe8b-d8e5-5080-9aa2-4142743a4b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7} \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392444} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cb1f592-094a-5b91-bac8-80971fe96ac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bde0cdd0-7dbe-5ba4-9b36-e66cfe099501" :question/math-academy-id "q-41949" :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7} & -3 & 3.5 \\\\ -2 & 2\\sqrt{5} & -2.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $B$ is the $1$st *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ by the $1$st *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3},$ the result must be the $1$st *column* of $A.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7} \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7} & -3 & 3.5 \\\\ -2 & 2\\sqrt{5} & -2.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 0+3.5\\cdot 0 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot 1+2\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 0+(-2.5)\\cdot 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7} \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115227 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8feeacff-b07d-5f98-a765-4d8571674a7a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Associative Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc44afbe-5d76-5e09-9981-f46801ba7f6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392450} {:answer/id #uuid "52743f64-5575-5f50-ab83-057fdf07b94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392451} {:answer/id #uuid "56be43e4-cbda-5ce6-b53d-23acb1dfe969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392452} {:answer/id #uuid "77068922-3e71-5bec-a56b-febc23200124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392453} {:answer/id #uuid "55501deb-48c2-5461-b4b5-3c1803f7a5f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc44afbe-5d76-5e09-9981-f46801ba7f6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392450} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb19f32f-0991-5b71-b98c-bdea9bdda5e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54c6b58b-7b12-5b44-8e47-3a85c2ee0cea" :question/math-academy-id "q-41981" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $4$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 11\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129082 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c61dc7c0-d3f8-5a52-a767-d5a5950ed61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ =-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128132} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3acfbd-450b-529e-9e81-f6898d0afc15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ =-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128133} {:answer/id #uuid "425120b8-9a7a-509b-b2b1-16f549aee0d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ = \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128134} {:answer/id #uuid "51495d9a-03f0-5f49-aeab-3c6ea5811fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128135} {:answer/id #uuid "681b517b-c0da-555d-9464-011b10f62e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ = \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128136}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a1f2f1dc-9d19-53e5-af38-cc9ea5f6ad94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128131} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2ab46d8-4b20-5cb0-bd03-d4843745957e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402771} {:answer/id #uuid "1281daa1-21d2-521b-ba7b-2f63be883923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402772} {:answer/id #uuid "cf49d4c9-aad2-5d7f-bca3-cd2582a8d854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402773} {:answer/id #uuid "1efc176a-2f08-5690-b21d-dbaa2aa9e6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402774} {:answer/id #uuid "9257c9b4-8a20-5a75-b76e-4b9c11a339a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1efc176a-2f08-5690-b21d-dbaa2aa9e6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402774} :answer-field/id #uuid "737f5b1b-3e66-5582-9362-63911dfedfe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "299c91a4-6289-5d24-ace1-495b52634a62" :question/math-academy-id "q-42043" :question/problem "![](images/91/919b9e8f2a9493fd0dda3e0d7bf66de9af054fc4ed9c0fcf7a68329fdbbaffdd.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2},$ what is the value of $tan⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/94/948610f67267e6ec1b845d2b4e1bc4030b81a1080bcb9a44d9f974ff78c3f7b0.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110568 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69d6ed5b-1c29-53a7-a37e-817042a02861" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85251810-38ab-5c23-8000-832183eefbba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e9/e97a7603936b6e125189e9868df34291e145aac878ca64c1c03761bf544fbbec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108728} {:answer/id #uuid "abdfb5b0-c85f-5799-b427-6af8ee1ed617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/32/326290611c8ca441f0d9bcc0439678f0c29302b2784ee4fe65e69a3008ec8fe3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108729} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd132f0-d5bb-5367-b1a4-75bd6fb41b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218